-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between ${u}_{1}^{′},{u}_{2}^{′},$ and $h(x):$\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-337736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-5x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y={y}_{p}$ and ${y}^{′}={y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+9{y}^{′}+20y & =h(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+9{y}_{p}^{′}+20{y}_{p} & =h(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-5x})^{′})}^{′}+9({u}_{1}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-5x})^{′})+20({u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}) & =h(x) \\\\ {(-4{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x})}^{′}-36{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}-45{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}+20{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+20{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x} & =h(x) \\\\ -4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}+25{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}-36{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}-45{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}+20{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+20{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x} & =h(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{(16{e}^{-4x}-36{e}^{-4x}+20{e}^{-4x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(25{e}^{-5x}-45{e}^{-5x}+20{e}^{-5x})}_{⏟}}_{0}-4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}=h(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\n-4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}=h(x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-337736" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43af5b5e073f788b6892d78fefe0bfca851fcc2333cab056e4cad4033e810668"} {"question" "q-337736" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-337736" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{u}_{1}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-5x})^{′}"] ["math" "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{-4x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{-5x})^{′}"] ["math" "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "{u}_{1}{e}^{-4x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-5x}"]] "cate