ath "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=0"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{3x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3(-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-5x}){e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ -5{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43af5b5e073f788b6892d78fefe0bfca851fcc2333cab056e4cad4033e810668"} {"question" "q-338433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338433" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-9y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-3{e}^{-3x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =-3-3 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-3x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}}{{e}^{-3x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-3(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{6x}){e}^{-3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 6{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int \\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338337" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43af5b5e073f788b6892d78fefe0bfca851fcc2333cab056e4cad4033e810668"} {"question" "q-338337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338337" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}{e}^{-3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-6y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{3x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{3x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(3{e}^{3x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}-3{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-5{e}^{x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{3x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}}{{e}^{-2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{5x}){e}^{-2x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 5{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43af5b5e073f788b6892d78fefe0bfca851fcc2333cab056e4cad4033e810668"} {"question" "q-338434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "2{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338434" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{-x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+2{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338349" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43af5b5e073f788b6892d78fefe0bfca851fcc2333cab056e4cad4033e810668"} {"question" "q-338349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x{e}^{x}"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}{e}^{x}"] ["math" "x{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}{e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}{e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338349" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}{e}^{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16y=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-4x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{1}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{4x})({e}^{-4x})^{′}-({e}^{4x})^{′}({e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{4x})(-4{e}^{-4x})-(4{e}^{4x})({e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =-4-4 \\\\  & =-8\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{4x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-4x})}^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{1}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{2}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}}{{e}^{-4x}}=-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{8x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}-4(-{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{8x}){e}^{-4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 8{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′} & =-2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{1}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{1} & =-\\int 2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)=2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338348" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "43af5b5e073f788b6892d78fefe0bfca851fcc2333cab056e4cad4033e810668"} {"question" "q-338348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "2{e}^{4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "4{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] ["math" "{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "q-338348" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/state.json" "file_sha256" "3e90059d58dacbbb3ef017f422fe7274bb0423c5ee3ee46cc65f43068bcb5835"} {"question" "e-21779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 5y' - 6y = p(x),\n$$\n\nwhose corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x)=e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/2e180f0d1ea06cd71caafeafb99628fdc8560253293ee5e9375dc84d5b735a64.png)\n\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/32e3388da577fa240b722e46a266b47356239cc79821d7479ed4ea43bd4875d4.png)\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between $u_1', u_2',$ and $p(x){:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/2876b8d3c2d6920da03a828ab411bb489bda9743cc0a210486c725348e14f13c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/example-21779.json" "file_sha256" "cdac34716e330a7c293160e844f120de26f60212506829d1ed714a7d0fd6abe3"} {"question" "e-21779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2 = e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1e^{x}+u_2e^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' =u_1(e^{x})'+u_2 (e^{-6x})'.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}-6y & =p(x) \\\\ ({y}_{p}^{′})^{′}+5{y}_{p}^{′}-6{y}_{p} & =p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for $y_p$ and $y_p',$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-6x})^{′})}^{′}+5({u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{-6x})^{′})-6({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}) & =p(x) \\\\ {({u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x})}^{′}+5{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-30{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}-6{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} & =p(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-6x}+36{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\,+5{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-30{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x}-6{u}_{1}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}{e}^{-6x} & =p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to $u_1$ and $u_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{{({e}^{x}+5{e}^{x}-6{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{2}{{(36{e}^{-6x}-30{e}^{-6x}-6{e}^{-6x})}_{⏟}}_{0}+{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}-6{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-6x}=p(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\nu_1'e^{x} - 6u_2'e^{-6x} = p(x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/example-21779.json" "file_sha256" "cdac34716e330a7c293160e844f120de26f60212506829d1ed714a7d0fd6abe3"} {"question" "e-21778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 16y = x\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\sin(4x)$ and $y_2(x) = \\cos(4x).$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/284b6c87b050df7b5663c21dcf357b1f2fa66a86f6c28604ab6681267394ada1.png)\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/f6f630f08ab3b335d43d12fa90c45df8fad0c73b83df793f43958e1ada7d199c.png)\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/32e3388da577fa240b722e46a266b47356239cc79821d7479ed4ea43bd4875d4.png)\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating $u_1'$ and $u_2'{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/assets/2876b8d3c2d6920da03a828ab411bb489bda9743cc0a210486c725348e14f13c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/example-21778.json" "file_sha256" "50007f0597b8a5bc74745ceb7c9430487701541a6e698e535fe8fa78390d12c9"} {"question" "e-21778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'.\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = \\sin(4x), \\qquad y_2 = \\cos(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1\\sin(4x)+u_2\\cos(4x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p',$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(\\sin(4x))'+u_2 (\\cos(4x))'.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'.$\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))}^{′}+{({u}_{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+{u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p',$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & ={u}_{1}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))^{′}+{u}_{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\cancel{4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\cancel{4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)} & =\\cancel{4{u}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}-\\cancel{4{u}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\nu_1' \\sin(4x) + u_2' \\cos(4x) = 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/example-21778.json" "file_sha256" "50007f0597b8a5bc74745ceb7c9430487701541a6e698e535fe8fa78390d12c9"} {"question" "e-6615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = 6\\cos x.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x).$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x,$ find the function $g.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/example-6615.json" "file_sha256" "4e9e916a374ff1f7460fa92c6fbd47471b15ef0c2dd51743313c2230b8cad777"} {"question" "e-6615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069266/example-6615.json" "file_sha256" "4e9e916a374ff1f7460fa92c6fbd47471b15ef0c2dd51743313c2230b8cad777"}] "usage" {"e-21779" [] "e-6615" [] "e-21778" []} "basis" 2358 "retractions" [[17592186147167 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y'' + 5y' - 6y = p(x)$,\nwhose corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions\n$y_1(x)=e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x)=e^{-6x}$.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x)$, that satisfies the constraint\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nSubstituting the expressions $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation yields the following relationship between $u_1', u_2'$, and $p(x){:}$\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$"] [17592186147167 :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2 = e^{-6x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1e^{x}+u_2e^{-6x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p'$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' =u_1(e^{x})'+u_2 (e^{-6x})'\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y = y_p$ and $y' = y_p'$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{″} + 5y^{′} - 6y &= p(x) \\\\\n(y_{p}^{′})^{′} + 5y_{p}^{′} - 6y_{p} &= p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our expressions for $y_p$ and $y_p'$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u_{1}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}(e^{-6x})^{′})^{′} + 5(u_{1}(e^{x})^{′} + u_{2}(e^{-6x})^{′}) - 6(u_{1}e^{x} + u_{2}e^{-6x}) &= p(x) \\\\\n(u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}e^{-6x})^{′} + 5u_{1}e^{x} - 30u_{2}e^{-6x} - 6u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}e^{-6x} &= p(x) \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}e^{x} + u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}^{′}e^{-6x} + 36u_{2}e^{-6x} \\\\\n+ 5u_{1}e^{x} - 30u_{2}e^{-6x} - 6u_{1}e^{x} - 6u_{2}e^{-6x} &= p(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we group all terms proportional to $u_1$ and $u_2{:}$\n\n$$\nu_{1}(e^{x} + 5e^{x} - 6e^{x})_(⏟)_(0) + u_{2}(36e^{-6x} - 30e^{-6x} - 6e^{-6x})_(⏟)_(0) + u_{1}^{′}e^{x} - 6u_{2}^{′}e^{-6x} = p(x)\n$$\n\nNote that the expressions in parentheses are both zero. Therefore, the equation reduces to\n\n$$\nu_1'e^{x} - 6u_2'e^{-6x} = p(x)\n$$"] [17592186147166 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y'' + 16y = x\\sin(2x)$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\sin(4x)$ and $y_2(x) = \\cos(4x)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\nwhere $u_1 = u_1(x), u_2 = u_2(x)$, that satisfies the constraint\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating $u_1'$ and $u_2'{:}$\n\n$AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA$"] [17592186147166 :question/worked-solution "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u_1(x)y_1(x)+u_2(x)y_2(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1y_1' + u_2 y_2'\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and $u_1$ and $u_2$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_1 = \\sin(4x), \\qquad y_2 = \\cos(4x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for $y_p$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p =u_1\\sin(4x)+u_2\\cos(4x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for $y_p'$, we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = u_1(\\sin(4x))'+u_2 (\\cos(4x))'\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates $u_1'$ and $u_2'$ by differentiating $y_p$ and equating this expression to our constraint on $y_p'$.\n\nDifferentiating $y_p$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p}^{′} &= (u_{1}\\sin (4x) + u_{2}\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= (u_{1}\\sin (4x))^{′} + (u_{2}\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + u_{1}(\\sin (4x))^{′} + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}(\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\n&= u_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for $y_p'$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= u_{1}(\\sin (4x))^{′} + u_{2}(\\cos (4x))^{′} \\\\\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) \\\\\nu_{1}^{′}\\sin (4x) + 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) + u_{2}^{′}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x) &= 4u_{1}\\cos (4x) - 4u_{2}\\sin (4x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\nu_1' \\sin(4x) + u_2' \\cos(4x) = 0\n$$"] [17592186147287 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$y'' + 9y = 6\\cos x$.\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions $y_1(x) = \\cos(3x)$ and $y_2 = \\sin(3x)$.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution\n$y_p(x) = u_1(x) y_1 + u_2(x) y_2$\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n${u_{1}^{′}y_{1}^{′} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2}^{′} = 6\\cos x; u_{1}^{′}y_{1} + u_{2}^{′}y_{2} = 0$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle u_2(x) = \\int g(x)\\,\\textrm d x$, find the function $g$."] [17592186147287 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 6\\cos x\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x)\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= y_{1}y_{2}^{′} - y_{1}^{′}y_{2} \\\\\n&= (\\cos (3x))(\\sin (3x))^{′} - (\\cos (3x))^{′}(\\sin (3x)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos (3x))(3\\cos (3x)) - (-3\\sin (3x))(\\sin (3x)) \\\\\n&= 3\\cos^{2} (3x) + 3\\sin^{2} (3x) \\\\\n&= 3 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent.\n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n{u_{1}^{′}(\\cos (3x))^{′} + u_{2}^{′}(\\sin (3x))^{′} = 6\\cos x; u_{1}^{′}\\cos (3x) + u_{2}^{′}\\sin (3x) = 0⟹{-3u_{1}^{′}\\sin (3x) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) = 6\\cos x; u_{1}^{′}\\cos (3x) + u_{2}^{′}\\sin (3x) = 0\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2$. We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3u_{1}^{′}\\sin (3x) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n- 3(- (u_{2}^{′}\\sin (3x))/(\\cos (3x)))\\sin (3x) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n3u_{2}^{′}(\\sin^{2} (3x))/(\\cos (3x)) + 3u_{2}^{′}\\cos (3x) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n3u_{2}^{′}(\\sin^{2} (3x) + \\cos^{2} (3x))/(\\cos (3x)) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\n3u_{2}^{′}(1)/(\\cos (3x)) &= 6\\cos x \\\\\nu_{2}^{′} &= 2\\cos x\\cos (3x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫u_{2}^{′}dx &= ∫2\\cos x\\cos (3x)dx \\\\\nu_{2} &= ∫2\\cos x\\cos (3x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
